---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Now" description="What I'm currently focused on." path="/now">
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">
		<header class="mb-4">
			<h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight" style="color: var(--color-text);">Now</h1>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Updated June 2026 · Boulder, CO</p>
		</header>

		<div class="mt-10 space-y-8 text-base leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--color-text);">
			<section>
				<h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Research</h2>

				<div class="rounded-lg p-5" style="border: 1px solid var(--color-border, rgba(127,127,127,0.2));">
					<div class="flex items-center gap-4">
						<img
							src="/images/edgeplane-logo.png"
							alt="EdgePlane logo"
							width="52"
							height="52"
							class="shrink-0"
							style="width: 52px; height: 52px;"
						/>
						<div>
							<strong class="text-lg">EdgePlane</strong>
							<div class="mt-0.5 text-sm">
								<a href="https://edgeplane.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2" style="color: var(--color-accent);">edgeplane.ai</a>
								<span style="color: var(--color-text-muted);"> · </span>
								<a href="https://github.com/RyanMerlin/edgeplane" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2" style="color: var(--color-accent);">GitHub</a>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<p class="mt-4">
						A production-grade control plane for multi-agent fleets. The driving question is what it
						takes to run agents with real operational rigor: the layer between a demo that works once
						and a system you can trust in production. Agents are first-class principals with stable
						identity, an inbox, and durable task state.
					</p>

					<p class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Built on a few core ideas</p>
					<ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 list-none text-[0.97rem]">
						<li>
							<strong>A control plane, not a pipeline.</strong> Most multi-agent systems are execution
							graphs that model what runs in what order. EdgePlane borrows the split that container
							orchestration settled on: a coordination layer that owns identity, desired state, and
							health, kept separate from the agents doing the work.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Persistent sessions as the unit of work.</strong> An agent is an addressable,
							resumable principal, not a stateless request. It can be steered while it is already
							working, attached to live, and restarted without pretending the prior run never happened.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Overlap detection before work begins.</strong> A fuzzy and vector similarity pass
							runs against the current state of a mission before any task or artifact is created, so two
							agents never quietly duplicate findings or collide on the same file.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Durable ownership that survives crashes.</strong> Every task and artifact has a
							stable owner identity rather than a session that evaporates on restart, so a recovered
							agent picks up exactly where the previous one left off.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>A governed publication path.</strong> Work products move from working state to
							operational state to a committed memory of record, with the full chain of custody preserved
							at each layer.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Missions accumulate, tasks complete.</strong> A mission is a perpetual coordination
							scope; a task is the unit of completion. The command surface and the node daemon mirror the
							kubectl and kubelet split.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 list-none">
					<li>
						<strong>Agent evaluation.</strong> Developing methods for measuring agentic system
						reliability in enterprise contexts, where failure modes are subtle and the cost of errors is
						real. Most existing evals assume clean inputs and well-specified tasks. Production rarely
						looks like that.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Forward Deployed Work</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-3 list-none">
					<li>
						Embedded with enterprise clients building production AI applications, shipping working
						systems rather than decks. Current engagements involve agent-native workflows in
						organizations navigating complex legacy infrastructure and real data governance
						constraints, codifying what works into repeatable deployment patterns. If you are building
						enterprise AI systems and want a practitioner in the room, <a href="/connect" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2" style="color: var(--color-accent);">reach out</a>.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Thinking About</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-3 list-none">
					<li>
						What the right abstraction layer for enterprise agent orchestration looks like, and why the
						gap between "it works in a notebook" and "it runs in production" is still so wide for most
						organizations deploying LLMs at scale.
					</li>
					<li>
						How frontier model capability improvements are reshaping the economics and architecture of
						agentic systems. The answer is shifting faster than most enterprise deployment patterns can
						keep up with.
					</li>
					<li>
						The organizational conditions that determine whether enterprise AI deployments compound or
						decay. High agency and the ability to navigate ambiguity in complex organizations matter as
						much as the technical implementation.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">Writing</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-3 list-none">
					<li>
						Publishing regularly on this blog. The focus is what I am actually building and deploying:
						agent infrastructure, secrets management, orchestration patterns, and the real failure
						modes. Field reports, not tutorials.
					</li>
					<li>
						Also active on <a href="https://x.com/rmerlin" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2" style="color: var(--color-accent);">@rmerlin on Twitter/X</a>
						for shorter takes and the AI builder conversation.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>
		</div>

		<p class="mt-12 text-sm" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
			Inspired by <a href="https://nownownow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2" style="color: var(--color-accent);">nownownow.com</a>.
		</p>
	</main>
</Base>
